  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1018 PM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nLittle change in the synoptic pattern is expected Tuesday with\nhigh pressure parked over the Great Basin. However, a few\nmesoscale developments will prove pivotal to the forecast. \nMonsoonal moisture will continue to trickle beneath the high \nTuesday afternoon. Height falls produced by weak shortwaves will\ninitiate a shift southeast of the high. In the meantime, this \nshortwave energy will engage the existing moisture pool and \nproduce another afternoon of convective showers and \nthunderstorms. Storms Monday evening were producing rainfall \nrates of 1-2 inches per hour in places, enough for numerous \nflash flood warnings on susceptible burn scars and flood prone \nterrain. There's reasonable confidence precipitation becomes \nmore widespread with additional dynamic forcing from the \nshortwaves. This should increase storm half- life appreciably \nover the next couple of days. Thereby, increasing the likelihood\nof more flash flooding and hydrological concern on burn scars. \nStorm motions will stay slightly eccentric on Tuesday favoring \neast to west motions once again.\n\nBy midweek, the high-pressure system will shift slightly,\naltering our storm tracks. While storms have recently been\ntaking an unusual east-to-west path, the new pattern will likely\nsteer them up from the south and perhaps the west by late week.\nAfter that shortwave passes to our north westerly flow becomes\nestablished. This may alter the moisture across the area, which\nin turn may change daily storm chances.  \n\nAs far as temperatures are concerned, daytime highs will\ncontinue to be above normal for the next few days, with near\nnormal temperatures expected Wednesday and beyond. \n\nA special note for outdoor enthusiasts: Please continue to\nmonitor the forecast and adjust your plans as needed. These\nstorms can develop rapidly, bringing sudden hazards. Your best\nbet is to head out early in the morning, as storm activity\ntypically strengthens in the afternoon. Always ensure you have\nmultiple ways to receive weather warnings.",
